{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a45c1a8560a69a48f","tool_use_id":"toolu_01AGWFHzQ6EuiqeeLRTJHdE3","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":69172,"tool_uses":19,"duration_ms":146400},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"5ab2e96a-0623-4fa1-83c7-a3628c8f7a4d","session_id":"89eb5c8b-0721-4f89-9e3a-11c1abe48cca"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GvmSgd1yjLeF6FERrNybNV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Qpu3dqg3KFszjjSEhcg3rK","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"irt_personfit_contam","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260520T092614","overall_score":"3 - Weak Reject","report":"# Reviewer R2: Novelty and Related Work\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper investigates whether IRT person-fit statistics (lz*) can detect benchmark contamination in LLMs using only binary response patterns. Through simulation, controlled SFT contamination experiments, and a 42-model leaderboard audit, the authors establish three negative findings: universal IRT model misfit renders absolute thresholds useless (93% flagged), SFT contamination is inseparable from genuine ability improvement (best AUROC=0.585), and an inverted-U power function creates a detection nadir at moderate contamination. The paper frames these as \"fundamental limits\" of binary-response detection and introduces a detection information hierarchy quantifying AUROC gains at each access level.\n\n## Strengths\n\n1. **Genuinely useful negative result at the conceptual level.** The core question — can person-fit statistics from psychometrics detect LLM contamination using only binary responses? — is well-motivated. The answer matters for the community because binary responses are the only universally available signal for retrospective auditing. Establishing that this operating point is insufficient has practical value for benchmark designers.\n\n2. **Clean experimental design for the controlled contamination study.** Two model families (Qwen3.5-9B, Gemma-4-31B), six contamination levels, three seeds each, with QLoRA SFT — this is a reasonable protocol. The observation that SFT knowledge transfer confounds detection (accuracy improves on non-contaminated items, theta absorbs the signal) is a genuine mechanistic insight that transcends the specific IRT framework.\n\n3. **The inverted-U power curve is a legitimate theoretical contribution.** Deriving and empirically confirming that detection power has a nadir at moderate contamination — where the aberrant signal transitions to an over-consistent signal — is a non-obvious structural property of person-fit statistics under contamination. This would be of interest to psychometricians as well.\n\n4. **The detection information hierarchy is a useful organizational framework.** Systematically ranking detection methods by information requirements (binary → re-test → token probs → logits → weights) and quantifying the AUROC gap at each level provides a conceptual scaffold that future work can build on.\n\n5. **Honest scoping.** The paper is upfront about the negative framing and does not oversell. The pivot from \"detection method\" to \"impossibility characterization\" is intellectually honest.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n1. **Severely inadequate related work — 7 references is far below the standard for a venue-quality submission.** This is the most critical weakness. The paper cites only 7 works, missing entire bodies of relevant literature:\n\n   - **IRT applied to LLMs**: Polo et al. (2024, tIRT/PSN-IRT — AAAI) is only obliquely referenced through the item parameters. Zhuang et al. (2024) on IRT-based LLM benchmarking is absent. Gu et al. (2024, \"How Well Can LLMs Negotiate?\") and other IRT-for-LLM works are missing. The ATLAS adaptive testing paper (arXiv:2511.04689) is not cited.\n\n   - **Person-fit statistics beyond lz***: The paper uses a single person-fit statistic. Karabatsos (2003) compared 36 person-fit statistics — this is a canonical reference that must be discussed. Why lz* and not HT, ZU, W, or nonparametric alternatives? Meijer & Sijtsma (2001) is cited but the discussion does not engage with the breadth of person-fit approaches.\n\n   - **Test security / item preknowledge literature**: The paper's entire premise is that \"a contaminated LLM is like an examinee with item preknowledge,\" yet the educational testing literature on preknowledge detection is barely engaged. Sinharay (2017, \"Which Statistic Should Be Used to Detect Item Preknowledge?\", JEBS) directly addresses the same power analysis question for human examinees and finds similar inverted-U phenomena. This is an extremely important omission — the inverted-U claimed as a contribution may already be partially characterized in the psychometric literature.\n\n   - **Other contamination detection methods**: Golchin & Surdeanu (2024, \"Time Travel\"), Yang et al. (2023, \"Rethinking Benchmark and Contamination\"), Zhou et al. (2023), Dong et al. (2024), Dekoninck et al. (2024, full ConStat paper), the CoDeC paper (ICLR 2026), and the Self-Critique paper (ICLR 2026) on post-RL contamination detection are either missing or barely mentioned.\n\n   - **Psychometric literature on non-human respondents**: There is zero discussion of whether IRT models are appropriate for LLMs in the first place. The finding that LLMs universally violate IRT assumptions (local independence, unidimensionality) is presented as novel, but this is well-known in the IRT-for-LLM community (e.g., Polo et al. discuss model misfit).\n\n   - **Negative results in ML**: The paper does not position itself within the tradition of negative results papers in ML (e.g., Lipton & Steinhardt 2019 on troubling trends, or the ML reproducibility crisis literature). Framing and contextualizing a negative result requires engaging with how the community has received and used such results before.\n\n2. **The \"first systematic investigation\" claim is likely unjustified.** The paper claims to be the \"first systematic investigation of IRT person-fit statistics for LLM contamination detection.\" This may be literally true in the narrow sense (no one has published exactly this combination), but the conceptual components — IRT for LLMs (PSN-IRT, tIRT), person-fit for cheating detection (decades of work), and contamination detection in LLMs (many methods) — are all well-established. The novelty lies in connecting them, which is a valid but incremental contribution. The \"first\" framing overstates it.\n\n3. **The \"fundamental limits\" framing is not supported by the evidence.** The title claims \"fundamental limits of binary-response contamination detection,\" but the paper tests exactly one family of statistics (lz* and its multiscale variant lzm) under one IRT model (4PL). The 3PL ablation shows negligible difference, but this does not explore: (a) nonparametric person-fit statistics that do not assume a specific IRT model, (b) multidimensional IRT models (MIRT) that could address the local independence violation, (c) group-level statistics (e.g., comparing distributions of responses across models rather than per-model lz*), (d) machine learning classifiers trained on binary response features. A true \"fundamental limits\" result would require an information-theoretic argument or exploration of a much broader class of methods. What the paper shows is that \"lz* under 4PL fails\" — a much narrower claim.\n\n4. **The leaderboard audit (42 models) is too small and the data coverage issue undermines the narrative.** Only 42 of 7,200+ models have usable per-item data. The paper acknowledges this but does not adequately address that the 42-model sample is likely severely biased (early adopters, specific model families). The conclusion that \"93% are flagged\" on 42 models cannot be extrapolated. Moreover, the project's own literature_map.yaml shows that the Open LLM Leaderboard v1 actually has per-item data for thousands of models — the paper appears to have used a much smaller subset than available.\n\n5. **The detection information hierarchy AUROC numbers are not directly comparable.** IRT-FIT's AUROC of 0.585 is measured on a different task (lz* shift between clean and contaminated models) than ConStat's 0.82 (original vs. rephrased performance) and Min-K++'s 0.78 (token probability distribution). These methods detect different signals on different inputs. Presenting them as a linear hierarchy from 0.585 → 0.82 → 0.78 implies a simple ordering that may not hold across contamination levels, model families, or benchmarks. The hierarchy is a useful heuristic but should not be presented as a quantitative law.\n\n6. **The post-RL analysis is underdeveloped.** GRPO is applied to one model at one contamination level. The claim that AUROC drops to 0.53 (random) is based on a single data point. The RL training details (2,000 steps, lr=1e-5, rule-based reward) suggest light RL — heavier RL (e.g., full RLHF, DPO with substantial data) could have different effects. The comparison with Wang et al. (2025) is directionally interesting but not rigorous enough to claim that binary-response methods are \"even more fragile\" than token-level methods post-RL.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you tested nonparametric person-fit statistics (e.g., HT, Guttman errors, U3) that do not assume a specific IRT model? Given that the 4PL model misfit is the core problem, model-free approaches might perform differently.\n\n2. Sinharay (2017, JEBS) derives power curves for preknowledge detection that show similar inverted-U patterns. How does your theoretical power analysis relate to that work? Is the inverted-U genuinely novel, or a known property reconfirmed in a new domain?\n\n3. The project's literature_map.yaml lists 10 papers including Karabatsos (2003), Sinharay (2017), and the Self-Critique paper (ICLR 2026). Why were these excluded from the paper's references? The current 7-reference list appears to be a severe undercount even relative to what the authors have surveyed.\n\n4. Why is the leaderboard audit limited to 42 models when the Open LLM Leaderboard v1 stores per-item prediction logs for thousands of models? The project files (memory.md) document that \"13,000+ models\" have data. If this is a data processing issue, it should be stated explicitly.\n\n5. Have you considered that the universal misfit finding — LLMs violate IRT assumptions — might itself be the more publishable and novel contribution, separate from the contamination detection question? The paper could potentially be restructured around \"why do LLMs violate IRT?\" rather than \"why does contamination detection fail?\"\n\n6. The Cohen's d of 0.25 is labeled as the contamination effect size. What is the test-retest reliability of lz* across the three random seeds? If within-condition variance is large relative to between-condition variance, this is a precision problem, not a fundamental information-theoretic limit.\n\n## Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract claims \"first systematic investigation\" without qualification. At minimum, add \"to our knowledge.\"\n- Table 5 (cross-benchmark correlations): Spearman rho of 0.18 average is described as evidence against cross-benchmark consistency, but with n=42, power to detect moderate correlations (rho=0.3) is only ~50%. The null finding may be underpowered.\n- The inverted-U nadir is reported at both \"25-40%\" (abstract) and \"~35%\" (Section 4) and \"~33%\" (Table 2). Consistency is needed.\n- The paper uses \"fundamental\" 8 times. This word should be reserved for proven impossibility results, not empirical observations from one method family.\n- Several references are incomplete (e.g., Wang et al. 2025 has \"arXiv:2502.xxxxx\").\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe core question is well-chosen and the controlled experiments are competently executed. The negative result — that lz* under 4PL does not separate SFT contamination from genuine ability improvement in binary responses — is a legitimate finding. However, the paper has three significant problems that prevent acceptance:\n\n(1) The related work is critically inadequate. Seven references for a paper that bridges psychometrics and LLM evaluation is unacceptable. Key works in person-fit statistics (Karabatsos 2003, Sinharay 2017), IRT for LLMs (multiple), and contamination detection (at least 5 additional methods) are missing. The inverted-U power curve, presented as a contribution, may already be partially characterized in the educational testing literature.\n\n(2) The \"fundamental limits\" framing vastly overclaims. Testing one statistic (lz*) under one model (4PL) does not establish fundamental limits of binary-response detection. The paper shows \"lz* under 4PL fails,\" which is a much narrower and less impactful claim. Without exploring nonparametric alternatives, MIRT, or providing an information-theoretic lower bound, the title and framing are misleading.\n\n(3) The leaderboard audit is underpowered (42 models) relative to both available data (thousands of models with per-item logs) and the claims being made. The 93% flagging rate on 42 models is not a credible estimate of ecosystem-level contamination.\n\nA major revision expanding the related work to 30+ references, narrowing the claims to \"lz* under 4PL,\" and scaling the leaderboard audit to hundreds of models would substantially strengthen the paper.\n\n## Confidence\n\n**4/5** — I am confident in my assessment of the novelty and related work gaps. I have familiarity with both the IRT/psychometric literature and the LLM contamination detection literature. My confidence is slightly reduced regarding the theoretical power analysis, where a psychometrics specialist might evaluate the derivation more precisely.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":738,"cache_read_input_tokens":68056,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":738,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01AGWFHzQ6EuiqeeLRTJHdE3","session_id":"89eb5c8b-0721-4f89-9e3a-11c1abe48cca","uuid":"971fc075-a2c8-4e47-81a8-66ae76ce7076"}
