{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AGWFHzQ6EuiqeeLRTJHdE3","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nThe review identifies three blocking issues:\n\n1. **Critically inadequate related work** -- 7 references is far below standard. Missing: Karabatsos (2003, 36 person-fit statistics comparison), Sinharay (2017, preknowledge power analysis with similar inverted-U findings), multiple IRT-for-LLM papers, at least 5 additional contamination detection methods, and the psychometric literature on non-human respondents. The inverted-U, presented as a contribution, may already be partially characterized in educational testing literature.\n\n2. **\"Fundamental limits\" framing overclaims** -- Testing one statistic (lz*) under one model (4PL) does not establish fundamental limits of binary-response detection. Nonparametric person-fit statistics, MIRT, group-level statistics, and ML classifiers on binary features are unexplored. The claim should be narrowed to \"lz* under 4PL fails.\"\n\n3. **Underpowered leaderboard audit** -- 42 models when thousands have per-item data available. The 93% flagging rate on a potentially biased 42-model sample cannot support ecosystem-level conclusions.\n\nKey strengths acknowledged: well-motivated question, honest negative framing, clean controlled experiment design, and the SFT knowledge-transfer confound as a genuine mechanistic insight.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-3/projects/irt_personfit_contam/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T092614/worst/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a45c1a8560a69a48f (use SendMessage with to: 'a45c1a8560a69a48f' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 72842\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 158200</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89eb5c8b-0721-4f89-9e3a-11c1abe48cca","uuid":"4a86617c-fee0-4576-bc40-e5b2ba1f99eb","timestamp":"2026-05-20T10:20:51.573Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"On the Fundamental Limits of Binary-Response Contamination Detection: Item Response Theory Person-Fit Statistics Applied to Large Language Models\"\n\nThe paper investigates whether IRT person-fit statistics (lz*) can detect benchmark contamination in LLMs using only binary (correct/incorrect) response patterns. Through simulation, controlled contamination experiments, and a leaderboard audit, the paper establishes three negative findings:\n1. LLM responses universally violate IRT assumptions, producing extreme lz* even for clean models (93% flagged under absolute thresholds).\n2. SFT contamination induces ability shifts inseparable from genuine improvement (Cohen's d=0.25, best AUROC=0.585, drops to 0.53 after RL).\n3. An inverted-U power function shows detection power reaches its nadir (≤0.12) at moderate contamination (25-40%)—exactly where detection matters most.\n\nThe paper frames this as characterizing fundamental limits of binary-response detection and introduces a \"detection information hierarchy\": binary (0.585) → re-test/ConStat (0.82) → token probs/Min-K%++ (0.78).\n\n## Paper Structure\n\n1. Introduction: Motivates IRT person-fit for contamination detection at the minimal-information operating point. Positions the work as the \"first systematic investigation\" of IRT person-fit for LLM contamination.\n2. Background: IRT (4PL model), person-fit statistics (lz* from Snijders 2001), detection methods hierarchy.\n3. IRT-FIT Framework: Uses PSN-IRT pre-calibrated 4PL parameters, MLE for θ, lz* computation, absolute vs. relative detection.\n4. Theoretical Power Analysis: Derives power from Snijders variance formula, finds inverted-U shape with nadir at π≈0.33.\n5. Simulation Study: 5 benchmarks × 8 contamination levels, two-sided detection.\n6. Controlled Experiments: Qwen3.5-9B and Gemma-4-31B, QLoRA SFT at 6 contamination levels, 3 seeds each. Key finding: SFT knowledge transfer confounds detection.\n7. Post-RL Analysis: GRPO further reduces signal to random-classifier level.\n8. Leaderboard Audit: 42 models from HuggingFace Open LLM Leaderboard v1. Universal misfit confirmed.\n9. Detection Information Hierarchy: IRT-FIT vs ConStat vs Min-K%++.\n10. Extended Analysis: BBH, MATH benchmarks; MMLU domain-level analysis.\n11. Ablation: 4PL vs 3PL; multiscale lz^m aggregation.\n12. Discussion: Three mechanisms (SFT knowledge transfer, universal misfit, inverted-U gap).\n13. Conclusion.\n\n## References in the Paper (7 total)\n- Brown et al. 2020 (GPT-3, NeurIPS) — n-gram overlap\n- De Koninck et al. 2024 (ConStat, NeurIPS) — rephrased re-testing\n- Hong et al. 2021 — multiscale person-fit statistics\n- Meijer and Sijtsma 2001 — person-fit methodology review\n- Shi et al. 2024 (Min-K%++, ICLR) — token-level detection\n- Snijders 2001 — asymptotic null distribution of lz*\n- Wang et al. 2025 (COLM) — MIA fails post-RL\n\n## Key Claims\n- \"First systematic investigation of IRT person-fit statistics for LLM contamination detection\"\n- Binary-response patterns contain \"insufficient information\" for reliable contamination detection\n- Establishes a \"detection information hierarchy\" quantifying AUROC at each access level\n- \"Fundamental limitation\" of binary responses for separating contamination from genuine ability improvement\n- Inverted-U detection gap is a \"structural weakness\"\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the core idea novel? Is the \"negative result\" framing genuinely new and valuable? Has similar work been done?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are important related works missing? The paper only cites 7 references—is this adequate?\n3. **Positioning**: Is the paper well-positioned relative to existing literature? Are the claims of \"first\" justified?\n4. **Contribution significance**: Does the paper advance the field? Would the community benefit from this work?\n5. **Framing and narrative**: Is the \"impossibility/fundamental limits\" framing justified by the evidence?\n\nConsider these potential gaps in related work:\n- IRT applied to LLMs more broadly (not just contamination)\n- Other person-fit statistics beyond lz*\n- Other contamination detection methods (e.g., benchmark rephrasing, data extraction attacks)\n- Psychometric literature on applying IRT to non-human respondents\n- Negative results in ML benchmarking\n- Test security and item pre-knowledge in educational testing\n- PSN-IRT (the source of 4PL parameters used)\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (numbered)\n- Weaknesses (numbered)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Minor Issues\n- Overall Assessment (score 1-6 with justification)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n","agentId":"a45c1a8560a69a48f","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nThe review identifies three blocking issues:\n\n1. **Critically inadequate related work** -- 7 references is far below standard. Missing: Karabatsos (2003, 36 person-fit statistics comparison), Sinharay (2017, preknowledge power analysis with similar inverted-U findings), multiple IRT-for-LLM papers, at least 5 additional contamination detection methods, and the psychometric literature on non-human respondents. The inverted-U, presented as a contribution, may already be partially characterized in educational testing literature.\n\n2. **\"Fundamental limits\" framing overclaims** -- Testing one statistic (lz*) under one model (4PL) does not establish fundamental limits of binary-response detection. Nonparametric person-fit statistics, MIRT, group-level statistics, and ML classifiers on binary features are unexplored. The claim should be narrowed to \"lz* under 4PL fails.\"\n\n3. **Underpowered leaderboard audit** -- 42 models when thousands have per-item data available. The 93% flagging rate on a potentially biased 42-model sample cannot support ecosystem-level conclusions.\n\nKey strengths acknowledged: well-motivated question, honest negative framing, clean controlled experiment design, and the SFT knowledge-transfer confound as a genuine mechanistic insight.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-3/projects/irt_personfit_contam/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T092614/worst/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":158200,"totalTokens":72842,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3644,"cache_read_input_tokens":68794,"output_tokens":403,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3644},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":403,"cache_read_input_tokens":68794,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3644,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3644,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":3,"searchCount":5,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a44eaedacc19023c8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sy3vsMup8JL9FLKE7o3nqe","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":112519,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":189862},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"d9ed6aad-bacf-4235-8279-8ccc461c97c8","session_id":"89eb5c8b-0721-4f89-9e3a-11c1abe48cca"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018o9n6sBfi2bKuLhW161EKe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0133THLygtERoABvJNyzwgwu","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260520T092614\ntier: worst\noverall_score: 3.0/6 — Borderline Findings\ntimestamp: 2026-05-20\n---\n\n# Dream Review Report: On the Fundamental Limits of Binary-Response Contamination Detection\n\n**Paper:** On the Fundamental Limits of Binary-Response Contamination Detection: Item Response Theory Person-Fit Statistics Applied to Large Language Models\n\n**Review Mode:** Full (2-Reviewer Roundtable)\n**Reviewers:** R1 (Methods + Experiments), R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThis paper investigates whether IRT person-fit statistics (lz*) can detect benchmark contamination in LLMs using only binary correct/incorrect response patterns. Through simulation, controlled contamination experiments on two models (Qwen3.5-9B, Gemma-4-31B), a theoretical power analysis, and a 42-model leaderboard audit, the paper arrives at a negative conclusion: binary responses are insufficient for reliable contamination detection. Three interacting failure modes are identified: (1) universal IRT model misfit (93% flagging rate, Type-I error = 0.42), (2) SFT knowledge transfer confounding detection (Cohen's d = 0.25, best AUROC = 0.585), and (3) an inverted-U detection gap with power nadir at moderate contamination. The paper reframes this as characterizing the \"fundamental limits\" of binary-response detection and provides a detection information hierarchy quantifying the AUROC cost at each information-access level.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Consolidated Strengths\n\n**S1. Genuinely novel conceptual connection (R1, R2 agree).** The bridge between IRT person-fit analysis (decades of psychometric work on exam cheating detection) and LLM benchmark contamination is new and intellectually well-motivated. The analogy that \"a contaminated LLM is functionally analogous to an examinee with item preknowledge\" is correct and has not been explored before. The detection information hierarchy (binary < re-test < token probs < logits < weights) is a useful organizing framework for the field.\n\n**S2. Honest negative result with mechanistic explanation (R1 primary).** The paper does not merely report that lz* fails; it provides a three-part mechanistic explanation for why. The SFT knowledge transfer analysis (Section 6.4) -- showing that QLoRA learns domain-level patterns rather than pure memorization, causing theta to rise and absorb the contamination signal -- is insightful. The direction inconsistency of Delta-lz* across contamination levels (positive at 15%, negative at 50%) and the model-capacity dependence (Gemma absorbs contamination more completely than Qwen) are informative findings that contribute to understanding SFT mechanics.\n\n**S3. Comprehensive experimental coverage (R1, R2 agree).** The paper covers simulation, controlled contamination at 6 levels on 2 models, post-RL analysis, leaderboard audit, baseline comparison, 4PL vs 3PL ablation, multiscale lzm, extended benchmarks (BBH, MATH), and domain-level analysis -- 12 distinct experiments. For a negative-result paper, this thoroughness is commendable and demonstrates that the conclusion is robust across multiple experimental axes.\n\n**S4. The inverted-U characterization has standalone value (R1).** The theoretical derivation of the inverted-U power curve -- where detection power is minimized at moderate contamination because the MLE theta estimate absorbs the contamination signal -- is a genuine theoretical contribution. The nadir formula and the observation that it occurs at pi approximately 0.33-0.35 (precisely the \"leaderboard gaming\" regime) has implications beyond IRT-FIT, as it applies to any ability-estimation-based detection method operating on binary responses.\n\n**S5. Detection information hierarchy is quantitatively grounded (R1, R2 agree).** Rather than merely asserting that gray-box methods are better, the paper quantifies the AUROC gain at each level: binary -> re-test (+0.235), binary -> token probs (+0.195). This provides actionable guidance for benchmark maintainers deciding what infrastructure to invest in.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Consolidated Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n**W1. The \"fundamental limits\" framing significantly overclaims relative to the evidence (R1 primary, R2 agrees).** The title claims to establish \"fundamental limits of binary-response contamination detection,\" but the evidence establishes only that one specific method (lz* under 4PL IRT with PSN-IRT parameters and QLoRA SFT contamination) fails. This is not an information-theoretic impossibility result. Several alternative binary-response approaches are not tested:\n\n- Simple accuracy z-scores across items (no IRT machinery needed)\n- Pattern-based anomaly detection (e.g., detecting items answered correctly that are answered incorrectly by all models of similar ability)\n- The Data Contamination Quiz approach (Ye et al., 2024), which operates at the same information level\n- Nonparametric person-fit statistics (Karabatsos, 2003) that do not assume the 4PL model\n- Residual-based methods that could be robust to the universal misfit problem\n\nThe failure of lz* under 4PL does not imply that ALL binary-response methods must fail. The paper's key finding -- that SFT produces knowledge transfer that raises theta -- is specific to the ability-estimation step in IRT, not to binary responses in general. A method that compares item-level residuals against a population of models (rather than against model-specific theta) might succeed. Without testing these alternatives, the \"fundamental limits\" claim is unsupported.\n\n**W2. The controlled experiment design has a critical confound: only 3 random seeds per condition (R1 primary).** With 3 seeds per contamination level, the standard error on Delta-lz* is enormous. The reported SE of approximately 7.5 means that even a true effect of Cohen's d = 1.0 would appear non-significant. The paper's conclusion that \"Delta-lz* is small and insignificant\" may be an artifact of extreme under-powering of the controlled experiment itself. A well-powered experiment would require at minimum 20-30 runs per condition to detect a medium effect size. The paper does not report a power analysis for its own experimental design, which is ironic for a paper about detection power.\n\nThis is not a minor concern -- the entire negative conclusion rests on the non-significance of Delta-lz* in Tables 4 and 5, which may simply reflect insufficient replications rather than a genuine null effect.\n\n**W3. The leaderboard audit is severely underpowered and the data availability claim undermines the paper's motivation (R1, R2 agree).** Only 42 of 7,200+ models have usable per-item data (0.6%). The paper frames IRT-FIT's advantage as \"operating on binary responses alone, which is the only signal universally available in archived evaluation logs\" -- but the audit reveals this data is NOT universally available. The 42-model sample is too small for meaningful temporal trend analysis (the reported p=0.34 for temporal trend confirms this), and the 93% flagging rate under absolute thresholds trivially follows from the universal misfit problem rather than revealing anything about contamination.\n\nThe cross-benchmark Spearman correlation of rho=0.18 (only 2/10 pairs significant) is presented as evidence against a stable contamination property, but with n=42, even a true moderate correlation (rho=0.4) would have only ~60% power to detect. The null finding is uninformative at this sample size.\n\n**W4. The comparison with ConStat and Min-K%++ is not on equal footing (R2 primary, R1 agrees).** The baseline comparison evaluates all three methods on the controlled contamination setup, but the ConStat and Min-K%++ numbers (AUROC = 0.82 and 0.78 respectively) appear to come from a different evaluation protocol than IRT-FIT's. Specifically:\n\n- IRT-FIT's AUROC of 0.585 is computed as the discriminability between clean and contaminated models using Delta-lz*. But what exactly is the classification task? Is it per-model (n=6 clean vs n=6 contaminated runs)? Per-item? The paper does not clearly specify.\n- ConStat's 0.82 AUROC -- is this from the original ConStat paper or from re-running ConStat on the same controlled models? If the former, the comparison is apples-to-oranges.\n- No confidence intervals are reported on any AUROC value, making it impossible to assess whether the gaps are statistically significant.\n\n**W5. The theoretical power analysis has limited practical value due to poor fit (R1).** The theory-simulation R-squared averages only 0.61 (range 0.51-0.68), and the theoretical curve overestimates power by 15-30%. The paper itself notes the closed-form bound is \"approximately 2x loose at moderate contamination.\" A theoretical bound that overestimates by 2x cannot serve as a practical calibration tool, and calling it a \"closed-form power function\" overpromises relative to what R-squared = 0.61 delivers. The poor fit is attributed to local independence violations in the contamination pattern, but this is precisely the real-world condition -- a theory that works only under assumptions known to be violated has limited utility.\n\n### Moderate\n\n**W6. Missing critical references (R2 primary).** The paper cites only 7 references in the bibliography. Critical omissions include:\n\n- Sinharay (2017) -- power analysis for person-fit under preknowledge, the most directly relevant prior work that partially anticipates the inverted-U\n- Karabatsos (2003) -- comparison of person-fit statistics, would justify (or challenge) the lz* choice\n- Ye et al. (2024), \"Data Contamination Quiz\" -- a same-information-level competitor\n- Lalor et al. (2019), Vania et al. (2021) -- IRT applied to NLP tasks\n- Golchin & Surdeanu (2024) -- contamination detection\n- Tendeiro et al. (2016) -- modern person-fit review\n- The PSN-IRT paper (Polo et al., 2024) is referenced but the bib entry appears inconsistent\n\nSeven references for an 8-page paper at a top venue is far below acceptable standards.\n\n**W7. The post-RL analysis is based on a single configuration (R1).** Only one RL algorithm (GRPO), one model (Qwen3.5-9B), one contamination level (15%), and one benchmark (MMLU) are tested. The claim that post-RL AUROC drops to 0.53 (random) is based on this single data point. DPO, PPO, KTO, and different RL training lengths could yield different results. The finding is suggestive but cannot support the general claim that \"binary-response detection is entirely washed out by post-RL training.\"\n\n**W8. The PSN-IRT item bank dependency is underacknowledged (R1, R2 agree).** IRT-FIT requires pre-calibrated 4PL item parameters from PSN-IRT, which were estimated from a specific set of 12 models. The information requirement is \"binary responses + calibrated item bank,\" not \"binary responses only.\" If item parameters are misspecified (e.g., calibrated on a non-representative model sample), lz* could be systematically biased. The paper does not investigate sensitivity to item parameter perturbations. This dependency should be discussed as a potential contributor to the universal misfit problem.\n\n### Minor\n\n**W9. The lzm null distribution claim is incorrect.** The multiscale statistic aggregates |lz*| values, whose sum does not follow N(0,1) under the null. The +0.015 AUROC improvement is within noise and the null distribution issue makes even this marginal result unreliable.\n\n**W10. Table density.** The paper contains 14 tables in approximately 8 pages of main text, which is excessive. Several tables could be consolidated (e.g., Tables 4 and 5 could be a single table; Tables 7 and 8 could be merged).\n\n**W11. The Gemini-1.5 case study (Section 10.2) is presented without adequate caveats.** Flagging 55/57 MMLU domains for a model that achieves near-perfect accuracy is entirely expected under universal misfit -- it does not constitute evidence for contamination. The paper acknowledges this (\"consistent with either comprehensive training data exposure or comprehensive domain knowledge\") but still foregrounds it as a finding.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Experimental power:** With only 3 random seeds per condition, what is the minimum detectable effect size for your controlled experiment? Have you computed the statistical power of your own experimental design to detect, say, d = 0.5 or d = 1.0? If the experiment is underpowered, the non-significance of Delta-lz* cannot support a \"fundamental limits\" conclusion.\n\n2. **Alternative binary-response methods:** Have you tested any binary-response detection method other than lz*? Specifically, a simple approach like \"accuracy on the hardest 10% of items relative to model ability\" or item-level residual analysis against a population baseline? If not, on what basis do you claim binary responses are fundamentally insufficient rather than lz* being a poor choice?\n\n3. **Item parameter sensitivity:** How sensitive is lz* to perturbations in the 4PL item parameters? If you recalibrate on a different set of models, do the results change materially? Could item parameter misspecification explain part of the universal misfit?\n\n4. **Adversarial relevance of the inverted-U:** Given that the power nadir at ~35% contamination is now published, could an adversary deliberately contaminate at this level to evade any future binary-response method? Does the inverted-U depend on the specific detection method (lz*) or is it a property of all ability-estimation-based approaches?\n\n5. **ConStat/Min-K%++ evaluation protocol:** Were ConStat and Min-K%++ run on your exact controlled contamination models, or are the AUROC values taken from their respective papers? If the latter, the comparison is not valid.\n\n6. **Leaderboard data:** The paper states only 42 models have usable per-item data. However, the Open LLM Leaderboard v1 results repository on HuggingFace appears to contain per-item logs for thousands of models via lm-evaluation-harness output. Can you clarify why your data extraction yielded only 42?\n\n---\n\n## 5. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\n| Experiment | Covered? | Quality |\n|---|---|---|\n| Extended Simulation (inverted-U) | Yes, Table 3 | Thorough |\n| Controlled Contamination Pilot | Yes, Table 4 | Underpowered (3 seeds) |\n| Full Controlled Contamination | Yes, Tables 4-5 | Underpowered; core weakness |\n| Analytical Power Theory | Yes, Tables 1-2 | Poor fit (R2=0.61) |\n| Leaderboard Data Recon | Yes, Table 6 | Reveals severe data gap |\n| Leaderboard Audit | Yes, Tables 7-8 | n=42, severely underpowered |\n| 4PL vs 3PL Ablation | Yes, Table 12 | Clean, supports conclusion |\n| Baseline Comparison | Yes, Table 9 | Unclear evaluation protocol |\n| Multiscale lzm | Yes, Table 13 | Marginal, null dist. issue |\n| Post-RL | Yes, Table 5 | Single configuration |\n| Extended Benchmarks (BBH, MATH) | Yes, Table 10 | Confirms negative result |\n| Domain-Level Analysis | Yes, Table 11 | Confirms granularity tradeoff |\n\n**Coverage: 12/12 experiments reflected.** Breadth is strong. Depth is limited by small sample sizes throughout.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Overall Assessment\n\n### R1 Score: 3.0 / 6 (Borderline Findings)\n\nThe paper's core contribution -- demonstrating that lz* under 4PL IRT fails to detect SFT contamination in LLMs -- is valid but narrow. The \"fundamental limits\" framing overclaims beyond what the evidence supports. The controlled experiment is critically underpowered (3 seeds), making it impossible to distinguish \"small true effect\" from \"no effect.\" The leaderboard audit (n=42) is too small to draw ecosystem-level conclusions. The theoretical power analysis has R-squared=0.61, insufficient for a \"closed-form power function\" claim. The detection information hierarchy and the inverted-U characterization are the strongest contributions, but both are diluted by the overclaiming in the framing.\n\n### R2 Score: 3.0 / 6 (Borderline Findings)\n\nThe novelty of connecting IRT person-fit to LLM contamination is genuine. However, the thin reference list (7 citations) misses the most relevant prior work (Sinharay 2017, Karabatsos 2003, Ye et al. 2024). The absence of any same-information-level baseline makes the \"fundamental limits\" claim unjustifiable -- the paper shows lz* fails, not that binary responses are fundamentally insufficient. The negative-result framing is honest but the scope of the negative conclusion is broader than the evidence warrants.\n\n### Consolidated Score: 3.0 / 6 (Borderline Findings)\n\n**Justification:** The paper investigates a well-motivated question (can IRT person-fit detect LLM contamination from binary responses alone?) and arrives at a negative answer through extensive experimentation. The inverted-U characterization and detection information hierarchy are genuine contributions. However, four issues prevent a higher score:\n\n1. **Overclaiming**: \"Fundamental limits of binary-response detection\" requires either an information-theoretic argument or exhaustive testing of alternative binary-response methods. The paper provides neither -- it shows one specific method (lz* under 4PL) fails.\n2. **Underpowered experiments**: 3 random seeds per controlled condition and 42 models in the leaderboard audit are insufficient to support strong negative claims. The paper's own non-significance findings may reflect low statistical power rather than true null effects.\n3. **Missing baselines**: No same-information-level comparisons (accuracy z-scores, Data Contamination Quiz, nonparametric person-fit) are provided.\n4. **Sparse references**: 7 citations miss critical prior work that would better contextualize the contribution.\n\n**Path to acceptance:** (a) Retitle to scope the negative result to IRT person-fit specifically, not all binary-response methods. (b) Increase controlled experiment replications to 20+ per condition to properly power the non-significance claims. (c) Test at least one alternative binary-response method to support (or narrow) the impossibility claim. (d) Expand references to 25+, including Sinharay (2017) and Ye et al. (2024). (e) Clarify the baseline comparison protocol and add confidence intervals. (f) Either expand the leaderboard audit (the data appears to exist for 1000+ models) or remove the ecosystem-level claims.\n\n**Verdict for EMNLP 2026 (~25% acceptance):** The negative finding is informative but the execution has significant gaps in statistical rigor and the framing overclaims. At a 3.0, this paper is at the Findings threshold -- potentially publishable in Findings with substantial revisions to the framing and experimental power, but not competitive for the main conference in its current form.","project":"irt_personfit_contam","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260520T092614","overall_score":"3.0/6 — Borderline Findings"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":774,"cache_read_input_tokens":111528,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":774,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sy3vsMup8JL9FLKE7o3nqe","session_id":"89eb5c8b-0721-4f89-9e3a-11c1abe48cca","uuid":"b678b958-132d-47da-bc30-f56a37fb0949"}
